-- CISSP Domain 7 expansion, part C (from-scratch coverage): 7.8 patch and vulnerability management (patch lifecycle, scanning cadence, testing/staging, emergency patching), 7.9 change management processes (CAB, change request lifecycle, rollback planning, emergency change), 7.10 recovery strategies - additional facets beyond RAID levels/backup types/hot-warm-cold basics already covered by ci-f-054/055/057 (backup rotation schemes, alternate site tiers, HA clustering), 7.11 disaster recovery processes (DR plan development, DR team roles, activation criteria, communication plans).

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 7.8 patch and vulnerability management (ci-q-1700..1714)
('ci-q-1700','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',1,$q$In the patch management lifecycle, what is the primary purpose of deploying a patch to a staging or test environment before pushing it to production systems?$q$,$q$["To confirm the patch installs cleanly and does not break critical applications or configurations before it reaches production","To permanently store a backup copy of the patch file for compliance audits","To automatically generate the patch's CVSS score","To notify the vendor that the patch has been received"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Staging exists to catch compatibility problems, application breakage, or performance regressions in a controlled, low-risk environment before the same patch is rolled out broadly to production, where an unexpected failure would have real operational impact. It has nothing to do with CVSS scoring, which is assigned by the vulnerability's discoverer or NVD, or with vendor notification.$q$),
('ci-q-1701','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',2,$q$How does an authenticated (credentialed) vulnerability scan differ from an unauthenticated (uncredentialed) scan?$q$,$q$["An authenticated scan logs into the target with valid credentials to inspect installed software versions, patch levels, and local configuration, while an unauthenticated scan only probes what is visible from the network without logging in","An authenticated scan only checks web applications, while an unauthenticated scan only checks network devices","An authenticated scan can only be run by an outside third party, while an unauthenticated scan can only be run internally","An authenticated scan encrypts its traffic while an unauthenticated scan does not"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Credentialed scanning logs into the host to directly enumerate installed packages, patch levels, and local misconfigurations, producing far more accurate and complete results than an uncredentialed scan, which can only infer exposure from externally visible network responses, banners, and open ports - useful for approximating what an outside attacker sees but far less thorough.$q$),
('ci-q-1702','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',1,$q$Which situation most clearly justifies bypassing the normal patch testing cycle and applying an emergency (out-of-band) patch immediately?$q$,$q$["A vulnerability with a published proof-of-concept is being actively exploited in the wild against systems the organization runs","A vendor released a minor feature update alongside routine bug fixes","The patch is scheduled to be released in the vendor's next quarterly update bundle","An internal scan found a low-severity informational finding with no known exploit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Emergency/out-of-band patching is reserved for vulnerabilities whose risk of exploitation is high and immediate, most clearly signaled by active exploitation in the wild (often reflected in CISA's KEV catalog) against software the organization actually runs - the urgency outweighs the normal testing cycle's risk-reduction value. Routine feature updates, scheduled bundles, and low-severity findings with no known exploit all fit the normal, tested rollout cadence instead.$q$),
('ci-q-1703','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',2,$q$A critical vulnerability is disclosed in a widely used web application framework, but the vendor has not yet released a patch. The security team configures a new Web Application Firewall rule to block the known exploitation pattern until a patch is available. What is this technique called?$q$,$q$["Virtual patching","Full disk encryption","Data remanence sanitization","Change freeze"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Virtual patching uses a compensating control, typically a WAF or IPS signature, to block the specific attack pattern for a known vulnerability at the network layer when no vendor patch yet exists, or when the real patch cannot yet be safely deployed - buying time without leaving the system fully exposed.$q$),
('ci-q-1704','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',2,$q$Under PCI DSS, how often must an organization at minimum run external vulnerability scans performed by an Approved Scanning Vendor?$q$,$q$["At least quarterly, and after any significant change to the network","Once, at initial system deployment only","Every five years","Only after a confirmed breach has occurred"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS requires quarterly external vulnerability scans by an ASV at minimum, plus additional scans after any significant change to the cardholder data environment - scanning cadence in general should scale with asset criticality and change frequency rather than being a single one-time or breach-triggered event.$q$),
('ci-q-1705','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',3,$q$A newly disclosed vulnerability has a moderate CVSS base score of 6.5, but it has been added to CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog and its EPSS score indicates a high probability of exploitation in the next 30 days. How should a risk-based patch management program treat this vulnerability relative to a different finding with a CVSS score of 9.0 but no known exploitation activity and a low EPSS score?$q$,$q$["Prioritize the KEV-listed, high-EPSS vulnerability for faster remediation despite its lower CVSS score, since real-world exploitation likelihood matters more than severity alone","Always patch strictly in descending CVSS score order regardless of exploitation evidence","Ignore the KEV listing since CVSS is the only authoritative prioritization signal","Delay both patches equally until the next scheduled quarterly maintenance window"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Modern risk-based vulnerability management combines severity (CVSS) with real-world exploitation evidence (KEV) and predicted exploitation probability (EPSS) rather than ranking purely by CVSS. A moderately-scored but actively exploited, high-EPSS vulnerability represents greater actual risk right now than a higher-scored vulnerability nobody is exploiting, so it should be remediated sooner.$q$),
('ci-q-1706','cissp',7,'7.8','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a typical patch management lifecycle, from the first activity performed to the last.$q$,$q$["Inventory affected assets and identify which systems require the patch","Assess and prioritize the patch based on severity, exploitability, and asset criticality","Test the patch in a staging/non-production environment","Deploy the patch to production according to a scheduled change window","Verify successful installation and monitor for post-deployment issues"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Patch management begins with knowing what needs patching (accurate asset inventory), then risk-ranking those patches, testing them in a controlled environment to catch breakage, deploying them through a controlled change window, and finally verifying the deployment succeeded and did not introduce new problems - skipping or reordering any stage, such as deploying before testing, increases operational risk.$q$),
('ci-q-1707','cissp',7,'7.8','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO conditions below that most strongly justify treating a patch as an emergency, out-of-cycle deployment rather than following the normal testing and scheduled rollout process.$q$,$q$["The vulnerability is being actively exploited against internet-facing systems the organization operates","The vulnerability allows unauthenticated remote code execution and no compensating control is currently in place","The patch also includes minor UI cosmetic improvements unrelated to security","The vendor bundled the fix with several unrelated low-priority feature changes"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Active exploitation against exposed systems and an unauthenticated remote-code-execution path with no compensating control both represent immediate, severe risk that outweighs the value of the normal testing cycle. Cosmetic UI changes or unrelated bundled features are irrelevant to urgency and do not, by themselves, justify skipping standard testing.$q$),
('ci-q-1708','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',1,$q$Why does a sound patch deployment process require a documented rollback plan before the patch is pushed to production?$q$,$q$["So the system can be quickly restored to its prior working state if the patch causes unexpected failures or breaks a critical application","So the vendor can be billed for the cost of the patch","So the patch's CVSS score can be recalculated after deployment","So the change can bypass the normal approval process entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Even a well-tested patch can behave differently in production due to configuration drift or edge cases not present in staging. A rollback plan defines exactly how to revert the change quickly, minimizing downtime and business impact if the patch introduces a new failure, rather than leaving the team improvising a fix under pressure.$q$),
('ci-q-1709','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary purpose of a centralized patch deployment tool such as WSUS or an enterprise configuration management platform?$q$,$q$["To distribute, schedule, and track patch installation status across many managed endpoints from a single console","To generate the CVSS score for newly disclosed vulnerabilities","To physically replace failed hard drives in a datacenter","To encrypt data at rest on managed endpoints"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Tools like WSUS (Windows Server Update Services) or broader configuration management platforms let administrators approve, schedule, push, and monitor patch compliance across large fleets of endpoints from one place, rather than manually patching each machine - improving both consistency and visibility into which systems remain unpatched.$q$),
('ci-q-1710','cissp',7,'7.8','matching',2,$q$Match each vulnerability scanning term on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Credentialed (authenticated) scan","Uncredentialed (unauthenticated) scan","False positive","False negative"],"right":["The scan only observes what is visible from the network without host login access","The scan fails to detect a vulnerability that is actually present on the target","The scan logs into the target host to directly enumerate software, patch levels, and configuration","The scan reports a vulnerability that does not actually exist on the target"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Credentialed scans authenticate to inspect internals directly, producing far more accurate results than uncredentialed scans limited to externally observable network behavior; separately, false positives waste remediation effort chasing non-issues while false negatives leave real exposure undetected - both scan accuracy failure modes that credentialed scanning helps reduce but never eliminates entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1711','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',3,$q$An organization has two systems affected by the same newly disclosed vulnerability: one is an internal file server with no external network access, and the other is a public-facing web server. Given limited patching resources this week, which system should be prioritized first under a risk-based patch management approach?$q$,$q$["The public-facing web server, because its network exposure gives a far larger population of potential attackers a path to exploit the vulnerability","The internal file server, because internal systems are inherently more valuable","Neither should be prioritized over the other since the vulnerability itself is identical","The system with the older hardware, regardless of network exposure"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Risk-based prioritization weighs exploitability in context, and internet exposure dramatically increases the realistic attacker population and likelihood of exploitation compared to an internal-only system reachable only by an attacker who has already gained a foothold. The underlying CVE may be identical, but the actual risk to the organization is not.$q$),
('ci-q-1712','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',1,$q$Before broadly deploying a patch across the entire production fleet, why do many patch management programs first push it to a small pilot or canary group of representative systems?$q$,$q$["To surface any unexpected compatibility or stability issues on a limited scale before the same problem could affect the entire fleet","To satisfy a legal requirement that all patches be tested on exactly one machine","Because pilot systems do not require any further monitoring after deployment","To reduce the total number of systems that will ever receive the patch"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A pilot/canary rollout limits the blast radius of any unforeseen problem the patch introduces to a small, monitored subset of systems, letting the team catch and fix issues before committing the change to the entire production environment - a practical middle ground between staging tests, which may not perfectly replicate production, and a full simultaneous rollout.$q$),
('ci-q-1713','cissp',7,'7.8','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe sound vulnerability scanning cadence and methodology practices.$q$,$q$["Internet-facing and business-critical assets generally warrant more frequent scanning than low-risk internal systems","A vulnerability scan should also be run after any significant infrastructure or application change, not only on a fixed calendar schedule","A single annual scan is always sufficient for every asset regardless of exposure or criticality","Uncredentialed scans alone provide the same depth of visibility as credentialed scans"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Scanning frequency should scale with an asset's exposure and business criticality, and significant changes such as new deployments or newly exposed services should trigger an additional scan outside the normal calendar cadence rather than waiting for the next scheduled cycle. A single annual scan is inadequate for any but the lowest-risk assets, and uncredentialed scans see meaningfully less than credentialed ones.$q$),
('ci-q-1714','cissp',7,'7.8','mcq',2,$q$How does the scope of patch management differ from the broader scope of vulnerability management?$q$,$q$["Patch management is the specific remediation activity of applying vendor updates to fix known flaws, while vulnerability management is the broader ongoing lifecycle of discovering, assessing, prioritizing, and remediating - via patches, configuration changes, or compensating controls - all forms of exposure","Patch management and vulnerability management are two names for the exact same process with no meaningful difference","Vulnerability management only covers hardware, while patch management only covers software","Patch management is performed only by external auditors"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Vulnerability management is the umbrella program: discovering exposures via scanning, assessing and prioritizing them, and remediating them through whichever method fits, which may be a vendor patch but could also be a configuration change, compensating control, or risk acceptance. Patch management is one specific remediation mechanism within that larger lifecycle, not a synonym for it.$q$),
-- 7.9 change management processes (ci-q-1715..1729)
('ci-q-1715','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary role of a Change Advisory Board (CAB) in the change management process?$q$,$q$["To review proposed changes, weigh their risk and business impact, and formally approve or reject them before implementation","To physically execute every approved change","To write vendor patches for approved changes","To perform the annual external financial audit"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A CAB is a cross-functional review body, often including IT, security, and affected business stakeholders, that evaluates a proposed change's risk, business impact, and readiness (testing evidence, rollback plan, scheduling conflicts) and decides whether to approve it - it does not itself implement changes or produce patches.$q$),
('ci-q-1716','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',2,$q$Which of the following must a well-formed Request for Change (RFC) document include before it can be meaningfully reviewed by a Change Advisory Board?$q$,$q$["A description of the change, its business justification, a risk/impact assessment, a testing summary, and a documented rollback plan","Only the name of the engineer requesting the change","The change's marketing budget","Nothing beyond a one-line summary, since details can be added after approval"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A CAB cannot meaningfully assess risk without knowing what is changing, why, what testing has already validated it, and how it can be undone if something goes wrong - an RFC missing any of these leaves reviewers unable to make an informed approval decision, which is exactly what a thorough RFC template exists to prevent.$q$),
('ci-q-1717','cissp',7,'7.9','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a standard (non-emergency) change request lifecycle, from the first step to the last.$q$,$q$["Submit a Request for Change (RFC) describing the proposed modification and its justification","Change Advisory Board reviews the RFC's risk, impact, and readiness","Approved change is scheduled into a defined change window","Change is implemented and validated during the change window","Post-implementation review confirms the change achieved its intended result and closes the record"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$A disciplined change process starts with formally documenting what is being requested and why, routes that request through risk/impact review and approval, schedules the approved work into a controlled window, executes and validates it, and finally closes the loop with a review confirming the change worked as intended - skipping the review or scheduling steps is exactly what turns a routine change into an unplanned outage.$q$),
('ci-q-1718','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',1,$q$What distinguishes an emergency change from a standard/normal change in change management?$q$,$q$["An emergency change addresses an urgent, time-critical need and is implemented through an expedited approval process, with full documentation and CAB review occurring after the fact","An emergency change never requires any documentation at all","An emergency change is implemented by an outside vendor exclusively","An emergency change always has lower risk than a standard change"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Emergency changes exist for situations, such as an active outage or a critical security exposure, that cannot wait for the normal CAB review cycle. They still go through a streamlined approval, often a smaller Emergency CAB or on-call authority, and are still fully documented and formally reviewed retroactively - an accelerated version of change control, not an exemption from it, and frequently higher risk, not lower, precisely because normal testing time is compressed.$q$),
('ci-q-1719','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',2,$q$Why does every significant change request need a documented rollback (backout) plan, even for changes that have already passed testing?$q$,$q$["Because testing cannot guarantee production behaves identically to the test environment, so a fast, pre-defined path back to the last known-good state limits damage if the change fails in production","Because rollback plans are only a legal formality with no operational value","Because the rollback plan replaces the need for testing altogether","Because rollback plans are only required for emergency changes"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Test environments rarely replicate production exactly, given data volume, integration points, timing, and configuration drift, so even a change that passed testing cleanly can behave unexpectedly once live. A rollback plan is the safety net that lets the team quickly restore service instead of troubleshooting live under pressure with no defined path back.$q$),
('ci-q-1720','cissp',7,'7.9','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO factors a Change Advisory Board would typically weigh most heavily when deciding whether to approve a proposed change.$q$,$q$["The change's potential business/operational impact if something goes wrong","Whether a documented rollback plan exists","The requester's personal seniority within the organization","The color scheme of the change request ticket"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A CAB's core job is risk management: assessing how much could go wrong and how bad that would be, and whether there's a credible way to recover if it does. Seniority of the requester and cosmetic ticket details are irrelevant to the actual risk being introduced into production.$q$),
('ci-q-1721','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',3,$q$A production database outage is actively affecting customers. The on-call engineer applies a configuration fix under the emergency change process without waiting for the full Change Advisory Board to convene. What must still happen after the fix is applied for this to remain a properly governed emergency change rather than an unauthorized change?$q$,$q$["The change must be formally documented and reviewed by the CAB, or Emergency CAB, after the fact, even though approval happened on an expedited basis beforehand","Nothing further is required once the outage is resolved","The engineer must revert the fix immediately regardless of whether it worked","The change only needs to be documented if a customer files a complaint"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An emergency change is still governed change, just on a compressed timeline - it requires expedited approval before or during implementation, often from a smaller emergency authority, and mandatory retroactive documentation and review afterward. Skipping that retroactive review is what turns a legitimate emergency change into effectively an unauthorized one, even if the technical fix itself was correct.$q$),
('ci-q-1722','cissp',7,'7.9','matching',2,$q$Match each change type on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Standard change","Normal change","Emergency change"],"right":["A low-risk, pre-approved, repeatable change following an established procedure that does not require case-by-case CAB review","A change that requires case-by-case risk assessment and CAB approval before scheduled implementation","A time-critical change requiring expedited approval and mandatory retroactive review"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Standard changes are pre-authorized templates for low-risk, repeatable work, like a routine account provisioning script, so requiring a fresh CAB review every time would be pure overhead. Normal changes are the default case-by-case path requiring assessment and approval before the change window. Emergency changes compress that approval timeline for urgent situations but add mandatory retroactive review to preserve accountability.$q$),
('ci-q-1723','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',2,$q$How does change management differ from configuration management in scope?$q$,$q$["Change management governs the process of requesting, assessing, approving, and implementing modifications to a system, while configuration management maintains the accurate, current record of a system's approved baseline state","Change management and configuration management are the same activity performed by different teams","Configuration management only applies to hardware, while change management only applies to software","Change management is optional, while configuration management is mandatory"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Change management is the governance process that controls how modifications get introduced through review, approval, scheduling, and rollback planning. Configuration management is the discipline of tracking and maintaining what the approved, current baseline actually is, often in a CMDB - the two work together, since every approved change should ultimately be reflected as an update to the configuration baseline, but they answer different questions.$q$),
('ci-q-1724','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',1,$q$What is the purpose of a scheduled change window (maintenance window)?$q$,$q$["To confine changes to a predefined, lower-impact time period, minimizing disruption to users and making it easier to predict and monitor for problems","To guarantee that a change will never fail","To eliminate the need for a rollback plan","To allow changes to be implemented without any prior approval"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A change window concentrates changes into a known, typically lower-traffic time period, such as a weekend overnight slot, so that any resulting issues are easier to detect and so unrelated changes aren't stacking up unpredictably during peak business hours - it reduces risk exposure and improves troubleshooting, but does not itself guarantee success or remove the need for other controls like rollback planning.$q$),
('ci-q-1725','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',3,$q$During a scheduled change window, the deployed change fails and the documented rollback procedure also fails to fully restore the prior state. What does this scenario primarily reveal about the change's original planning?$q$,$q$["The rollback plan was not adequately tested or validated before the change window, since a sound plan should be verified capable of restoring the prior state","The change should never have been attempted at all, regardless of testing","This outcome is unavoidable and reveals nothing about planning quality","The CAB should have skipped reviewing the rollback plan entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A rollback plan's entire value depends on it actually working when needed - ideally it should be tested or at least carefully validated during the review/testing phase, not assumed to work. A rollback failure during a live change window points to a gap in that validation, and it is a strong argument for why rollback procedures deserve the same scrutiny as the forward change itself, not less.$q$),
('ci-q-1726','cissp',7,'7.9','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO reasons an unauthorized change, one implemented without going through the change management process, is considered especially risky.$q$,$q$["It bypasses risk assessment, so its potential impact was never evaluated before implementation","There is no documented rollback plan or tested recovery path if it causes a failure","Unauthorized changes are always technically incorrect","Unauthorized changes are automatically detected and blocked by every firewall"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The danger of an unauthorized change is procedural, not necessarily technical: nobody assessed its risk or impact beforehand, and there's typically no vetted rollback plan if it goes wrong, meaning problems surface unexpectedly and recovery is improvised. The change might even be technically sound; the risk is that its consequences were never evaluated or planned for.$q$),
('ci-q-1727','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',1,$q$In many organizations' terminology, how does a Change Control Board (CCB) typically relate to a Change Advisory Board (CAB)?$q$,$q$["They generally describe the same governing function - reviewing and approving proposed changes - with the specific term used varying by organization or framework","A CCB only approves changes to marketing materials","A CAB has no approval authority and only offers non-binding suggestions","A CCB exists solely within emergency change procedures"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CCB and CAB are largely interchangeable terms for the same governance body across different organizational or framework conventions, with CCB more common in project/systems-engineering contexts and CAB in ITIL-derived IT service management. Both perform the same core function of reviewing and approving proposed changes before implementation.$q$),
('ci-q-1728','cissp',7,'7.9','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary purpose of conducting a post-implementation review after a change is deployed?$q$,$q$["To confirm the change achieved its intended result without unexpected side effects, and to capture lessons learned for future changes","To retroactively deny approval for a change that has already succeeded","To bill the requester for the cost of the change","To determine whether the RFC form was filled out in the correct font"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A post-implementation review closes the loop on the change management lifecycle by verifying the change actually worked as planned, checking for any unexpected side effects, and feeding lessons learned back into the process - it is a quality and continuous-improvement step, not a formality.$q$),
('ci-q-1729','cissp',7,'7.9','ordering',3,$q$Order the stages of an emergency change process, from the first action taken to the last.$q$,$q$["An urgent, time-critical need for the change is identified, such as an active outage or actively exploited vulnerability","Expedited approval is obtained from a limited emergency authority, such as an on-call manager or Emergency CAB","The change is implemented immediately to resolve the urgent condition","The change is formally documented and reported to the full Change Advisory Board","A retroactive review is conducted to confirm the change was appropriate and capture lessons learned"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Emergency change control still follows a defined order: recognize the urgent trigger, obtain fast but real approval from an authority empowered to grant it, implement the fix, then catch the formal record-keeping and full board review up after the fact - the retroactive steps are what keep an emergency change accountable rather than simply undocumented.$q$),
-- 7.10 recovery strategies (ci-q-1730..1743) - RAID levels 0/1/5/6/10, full/incremental/differential backups, and hot/warm/cold sites are already covered by ci-f-054/055/057 and ci-q-139; this set covers additional facets.
('ci-q-1730','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',1,$q$What is the defining characteristic of the Grandfather-Father-Son (GFS) backup rotation scheme?$q$,$q$["It layers backups at daily (son), weekly (father), and monthly (grandfather) intervals, retaining each tier for progressively longer periods so recent data has frequent restore points while older data is retained more sparsely","It requires a completely new, unique tape for every single backup ever taken, with no reuse","It only ever performs full backups, never incremental or differential","It eliminates the need for offsite storage of any backup media"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$GFS is a tiered rotation scheme: daily ("son") backups are kept briefly, the last backup of the week is promoted to a weekly ("father") set kept longer, and the last backup of the month is promoted to a monthly ("grandfather") set kept longest - balancing frequent recent restore points against the storage cost of retaining data at full daily granularity indefinitely.$q$),
('ci-q-1731','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',2,$q$How does the Tower of Hanoi backup rotation scheme differ from a simple GFS scheme in how it reuses backup media?$q$,$q$["It reuses a smaller number of media sets in a mathematically staggered pattern, where some sets are reused far more frequently than others, extending the effective retention window without proportionally increasing the number of media sets needed","It never reuses media under any circumstances","It requires exactly the same number of media sets as there are days in a year","It only supports full backups and cannot be combined with incremental backups"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Modeled on the mathematical Tower of Hanoi puzzle, this scheme assigns backup media to sets that get reused at exponentially staggered intervals, some far more often than others, which extends how far back in time a restore can reach using fewer total media sets than a straightforward daily rotation would require.$q$),
('ci-q-1732','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',2,$q$What is the key difference between electronic vaulting and remote journaling as offsite data protection techniques?$q$,$q$["Electronic vaulting transfers batches of backup data to an offsite location on a scheduled interval, while remote journaling transmits transaction-level changes to the offsite location in near-real time as they occur","Electronic vaulting is only used for paper records, while remote journaling is only used for digital records","Remote journaling requires physically shipping tapes offsite, while electronic vaulting does not","They are two names for the exact same technique with no functional difference"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Electronic vaulting bundles and transfers backup data to an offsite repository on a scheduled batch basis, while remote journaling continuously ships individual transaction log entries to the offsite location as they happen, giving it a much smaller potential data-loss window, a lower RPO, than batch-based vaulting.$q$),
('ci-q-1733','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',1,$q$How does a mirrored site differ from a hot site as an alternate processing strategy?$q$,$q$["A mirrored site maintains a continuously, synchronously updated exact duplicate of production ready for essentially instantaneous failover, whereas a hot site is fully equipped but typically restores from near-real-time replicated or recent backup data with some recovery time still required","A mirrored site has no equipment installed, only floor space and power","A hot site can only be activated once every calendar year","A mirrored site and a hot site are identical in every respect"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Both are on the fast-recovery end of the spectrum, but a mirrored site goes further than a typical hot site by keeping a live, synchronously updated duplicate environment running continuously, enabling failover in minutes or less with essentially zero data loss - at a correspondingly higher ongoing cost than a hot site that still requires some activation and data-catch-up time.$q$),
('ci-q-1734','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',3,$q$What is a significant practical limitation of relying on a reciprocal agreement with another organization as an alternate processing site strategy?$q$,$q$["The partner organization's own capacity may be insufficient or unavailable if both organizations are affected by the same regional disaster at the same time","Reciprocal agreements are illegal in most jurisdictions","Reciprocal agreements guarantee unlimited capacity regardless of circumstances","Reciprocal agreements eliminate the need for any BIA or RTO planning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A reciprocal agreement, where two organizations agree to host each other's operations in a disaster, sounds economical but breaks down precisely when a widespread disaster, such as a regional flood, earthquake, or power grid failure, affects both parties simultaneously, leaving neither with spare capacity to lend the other - a risk that's easy to overlook when the agreement is signed under normal conditions.$q$),
('ci-q-1735','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',1,$q$What is a key advantage of Disaster-Recovery-as-a-Service (DRaaS), a cloud-based recovery strategy, compared to maintaining a dedicated physical hot site?$q$,$q$["It lets an organization pay for recovery infrastructure on a flexible, often consumption-based model instead of maintaining and funding a fully equipped physical facility year-round whether or not it is ever used","It guarantees zero recovery time under all circumstances","It removes any need to define RTO or RPO targets","It is only available to organizations with no internet connectivity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$DRaaS shifts the cost model from continuously funding dedicated physical infrastructure to a more elastic, often usage-based cloud arrangement, which can substantially reduce the fixed cost of maintaining a rarely-used hot site - though the organization still must define its RTO/RPO targets and verify the provider's capabilities actually meet them.$q$),
('ci-q-1736','cissp',7,'7.10','ordering',2,$q$Order these recovery/backup approaches from the LARGEST typical potential data loss window (highest RPO) to the SMALLEST (lowest RPO, most current data).$q$,$q$["Traditional nightly full backup to tape","Nightly full backup supplemented by periodic incremental backups during the day","Continuous Data Protection (CDP) capturing every write as it happens","Synchronous real-time replication to a mirrored site"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$A single nightly full backup can lose up to a full day of changes. Adding periodic intraday incrementals shrinks that window. CDP captures essentially every write continuously, shrinking the window further to near-zero. Synchronous replication to a mirrored site writes to both locations simultaneously before the transaction is even considered complete, giving the lowest possible RPO of the four - progressively more current data comes at progressively higher infrastructure cost and complexity.$q$),
('ci-q-1737','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',2,$q$In a failover cluster, what is a "split-brain" condition?$q$,$q$["A scenario where a communication failure between cluster nodes causes each side to independently believe it is the sole active node, potentially leading both to write data simultaneously and corrupt shared state","A scenario where a cluster node is deliberately taken offline for scheduled maintenance","A scenario where a cluster has too few nodes to function at all","A scenario where a cluster automatically balances load evenly across all nodes with no issues"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Split-brain occurs when cluster nodes lose the heartbeat/communication link that lets them agree on which node is authoritative, and, unable to confirm the other side's status, each concludes it should become active - if both then accept writes independently, the result is diverging, inconsistent, or corrupted shared data once connectivity is restored.$q$),
('ci-q-1738','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',1,$q$What is the difference between an active-active and an active-passive high-availability cluster configuration?$q$,$q$["In active-active, all nodes handle production workload simultaneously, sharing the load; in active-passive, one node handles all production traffic while a standby node remains idle, ready to take over only if the active node fails","Active-active clusters have no redundancy at all","Active-passive clusters process twice as much traffic as active-active clusters","There is no meaningful difference between the two terms"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Active-active maximizes resource utilization by having every node actively serve traffic, with the cluster tolerating the loss of any one node by redistributing load to the rest, while active-passive keeps a standby node fully idle until a failover event, trading unused capacity for a simpler, more predictable failover model.$q$),
('ci-q-1739','cissp',7,'7.10','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO benefits of an N+1 redundancy design (one additional unit beyond the minimum required, such as N+1 power supplies or cluster nodes) compared to a design with exactly N units and no spare capacity.$q$,$q$["The system can tolerate the failure of one unit while continuing to operate at full required capacity","Maintenance can be performed on one unit without dropping below the minimum required capacity","N+1 designs eliminate the possibility of any future failure entirely","N+1 designs always cost less than an N-only design"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$N+1 provisions exactly one spare unit beyond what's minimally needed, so a single failure, or planned maintenance on one unit, doesn't drop the system below its required operating capacity. It does not make failure impossible, since a second simultaneous failure would still cause a shortfall, and it costs more than an N-only design, not less, in exchange for that resilience.$q$),
('ci-q-1740','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',3,$q$A database team is choosing a RAID level for a new server supporting a write-heavy transactional database. Why might they choose RAID 10 over RAID 5 despite RAID 10 delivering less usable storage capacity from the same number of drives?$q$,$q$["RAID 5 must calculate and write parity data on every write operation, creating a write performance penalty that RAID 10's mirrored-and-striped design avoids, making RAID 10 better suited to write-intensive workloads","RAID 10 is always cheaper per usable gigabyte than RAID 5","RAID 5 cannot survive the failure of any drive, unlike RAID 10","RAID 10 requires fewer physical drives than RAID 5 for the same usable capacity"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$RAID 5's distributed parity must be recalculated and written on every write, a known performance cost commonly called the "write penalty," which becomes significant under heavy write load. RAID 10 mirrors and stripes data without parity calculations, so it sustains much higher write throughput - a common reason a write-heavy database opts for RAID 10 despite it using more raw drive capacity per usable gigabyte, not less.$q$),
('ci-q-1741','cissp',7,'7.10','matching',2,$q$Match each backup/recovery concept on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Continuous Data Protection (CDP)","Storage snapshot","Synthetic full backup","WORM media"],"right":["Captures every write to storage continuously, enabling recovery to nearly any point in time","A point-in-time image of a storage volume's state, typically created quickly using copy-on-write techniques","A full backup constructed by combining a prior full backup with subsequent incremental backups, without re-reading all data from the source system again","Storage media that can be written once and then only read, protecting stored backups from later modification or deletion"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$CDP protects against nearly any point-in-time loss by capturing writes continuously; a snapshot is a fast, point-in-time capture of a volume's state; a synthetic full backup saves time and source-system load by assembling a full backup from previously captured full and incremental data rather than reading the entire dataset from the source again; and Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) media protects already-written backups from tampering or deletion, which matters for both integrity and certain regulatory retention requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-1742','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',1,$q$What does the "3-2-1" backup rule recommend?$q$,$q$["Keep at least 3 copies of data, on at least 2 different types of media, with at least 1 copy stored offsite","Perform backups 3 times per day, 2 days per week, for 1 year","Retain backups for exactly 3 years, using 2 backup administrators, at 1 location","Use 3 different encryption algorithms, 2 access control methods, and 1 password policy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The 3-2-1 rule is a simple, widely taught backup resilience heuristic: maintain at least three total copies of important data, store them on at least two different media types, reducing the chance a single media-type failure destroys every copy, and keep at least one copy offsite, protecting against a site-wide disaster destroying every local copy at once.$q$),
('ci-q-1743','cissp',7,'7.10','mcq',3,$q$A failover cluster is configured so that no node can unilaterally declare itself active after losing contact with the other nodes unless it can also communicate with a separate quorum witness resource. What problem is this design specifically intended to prevent?$q$,$q$["Split-brain, by requiring a majority/tie-breaking vote before any node assumes sole authority over the cluster's shared data","Data encryption key loss","Excessive power consumption during normal operation","Slow initial cluster setup time"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A quorum or witness mechanism forces cluster nodes to reach a majority agreement, including the tie-breaking witness vote, before any single node can act as the authoritative active node, specifically preventing the split-brain scenario where multiple isolated nodes each independently assume control and issue conflicting writes to shared storage.$q$),
-- 7.11 disaster recovery processes (ci-q-1744..1758)
('ci-q-1744','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary focus of a Disaster Recovery Plan (DRP), as distinct from a Business Continuity Plan's broader scope?$q$,$q$["Restoring IT systems, infrastructure, and data to a functioning state after a disruptive event, within defined recovery time and point objectives","Managing the organization's public marketing strategy during a crisis","Setting annual employee performance goals","Negotiating vendor contracts unrelated to recovery"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The DRP is the technology-recovery-focused component that operationalizes getting systems and data back online within defined RTO/RPO targets, while the broader business continuity plan covers keeping essential business functions running, which may span people, facilities, and processes well beyond IT - the DRP is typically a supporting sub-plan feeding into that larger continuity effort.$q$),
('ci-q-1745','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$Which outputs from the Business Impact Analysis (BIA) most directly feed into developing a disaster recovery plan?$q$,$q$["The list of critical systems, their Recovery Time Objectives (RTO), Recovery Point Objectives (RPO), and system interdependencies","The company's annual marketing budget","The organization's employee vacation policy","The building's fire insurance premium amount"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The BIA identifies which systems are critical, how quickly they must be restored, how much data loss is tolerable, and which systems depend on which others - these outputs directly shape which systems the DR plan must prioritize, in what order, and to what recovery targets.$q$),
('ci-q-1746','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary responsibility of a Damage Assessment Team during disaster recovery?$q$,$q$["Evaluating the extent of damage to facilities, equipment, and systems to determine what can be salvaged, what must be replaced, and how the recovery scope should be scaled","Approving the annual IT security budget","Writing new application source code during the outage","Handling media and press inquiries"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Damage Assessment Team's job is to quickly and accurately determine what was actually affected and how badly, which directly informs the scale of the recovery effort, such as a partial facility recovery versus full alternate-site activation - decisions that should not be made blindly before this assessment is complete.$q$),
('ci-q-1747','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',2,$q$What does a Salvage Team typically do during the disaster recovery process?$q$,$q$["Attempts to recover, clean, and restore damaged equipment, media, and records for reuse, and coordinates the safe return to the primary facility once it is habitable again","Designs the organization's long-term IT architecture roadmap","Conducts the initial fire evacuation of personnel","Sets vulnerability scanning cadence for the following year"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Salvage Team focuses on recovering value from what was damaged, restoring or safely disposing of affected equipment and media, and later coordinating the return-to-normal-operations transition back to the primary site - distinct from the initial emergency response or the ongoing operation of recovery systems at an alternate site.$q$),
('ci-q-1748','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$What is the primary focus of the Emergency Response Team in the earliest moments of a disaster?$q$,$q$["Life safety - evacuating personnel and responding to the immediate physical emergency, such as fire, structural danger, or medical needs, before recovery activities begin","Restoring the payroll database","Reviewing quarterly vulnerability scan results","Drafting the post-incident lessons-learned report"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The Emergency Response Team's mandate is the immediate physical safety of people - evacuation, first aid, coordinating with fire/medical responders - which always takes priority over any IT or business recovery activity; recovery teams engage only once the immediate life-safety emergency is under control.$q$),
('ci-q-1749','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$What is the core responsibility of a DR Coordinator (or DR Manager) role within the overall disaster recovery organization?$q$,$q$["Owning the DR plan's currency, coordinating the various recovery teams' activities, and serving as the central point driving execution of the plan during an actual event","Physically repairing damaged network cabling","Performing the organization's external financial audit","Approving routine, non-emergency software feature requests"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The DR Coordinator is the person, or role, responsible for keeping the DR plan accurate and tested, and for orchestrating the various specialized teams, such as damage assessment, salvage, and technical recovery, during an actual activation so their efforts stay coordinated toward the plan's recovery objectives rather than working at cross purposes.$q$),
('ci-q-1750','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',3,$q$Why does an effective disaster recovery plan define specific, predetermined activation/declaration criteria in advance, rather than leaving the decision to whoever is present when an incident begins?$q$,$q$["Predefined criteria, such as expected outage duration relative to RTO or extent of physical damage, prevent both under-reaction, waiting too long to activate and extending downtime, and over-reaction, declaring a disaster for an incident that would resolve on its own","Predefined criteria remove the need for any human judgment during an actual event","Predefined criteria are a purely legal formality with no operational value","Predefined criteria only matter for emergency changes, not disaster recovery"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Without clear, objective thresholds decided calmly in advance, whoever is present during an actual crisis is left guessing under pressure - risking either activating a costly, disruptive alternate-site failover for something that would have resolved on its own, or delaying activation past the point where the RTO can still be met. Predefined criteria give that decision-maker an objective basis to act quickly and confidently.$q$),
('ci-q-1751','cissp',7,'7.11','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a disaster recovery plan activation, from the first step to the last.$q$,$q$["An incident affecting systems or facilities is detected","Damage assessment is performed to determine scope and severity","The designated activation authority formally declares a disaster based on predefined criteria","Recovery teams and affected stakeholders are notified according to the communication plan","Recovery procedures are executed to restore operations, followed by a return to normal operations once safe"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$DR activation proceeds from detecting a problem, assessing how severe it actually is, formally declaring a disaster against predefined criteria rather than reacting ad hoc, notifying the people and teams who need to act, and finally executing the actual recovery work through to a controlled return to normal operations - skipping the assessment or declaration steps risks either overreacting or activating recovery procedures without a clear, authorized trigger.$q$),
('ci-q-1752','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',2,$q$Why is it important that a specific, named individual or role, not "whoever is available," holds the authority to formally declare a disaster and activate the DR plan?$q$,$q$["A clearly designated activation authority ensures a timely, accountable decision is made against the predefined criteria, instead of the decision being delayed by uncertainty over who is empowered to make it","Only the CEO is legally permitted to ever declare any type of emergency","Designating an authority removes the need for predefined activation criteria","It ensures the decision can always be delegated to an outside vendor"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$During a real incident, ambiguity over who can actually pull the trigger on a disaster declaration causes exactly the kind of delay that predefined criteria are meant to prevent - having a clearly designated authority, often the DR Coordinator or a senior executive, ensures someone with the standing and information to decide can act promptly and be held accountable for that decision.$q$),
('ci-q-1753','cissp',7,'7.11','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO elements that are essential to an effective communication plan used during a disaster recovery event.$q$,$q$["A pre-established method, such as a call tree or automated emergency notification system, for reaching personnel and stakeholders without depending on normal, potentially disrupted communication channels","A clearly designated spokesperson responsible for consistent external/media communications","A requirement that all communication happen exclusively through the primary office's landline phones","A rule that no communication with staff occurs until the disaster is fully resolved"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An effective DR communication plan assumes normal channels, such as the affected office's phones or internal email hosted on now-down systems, may themselves be unavailable, so it needs a redundant, pre-established notification method, and it needs a single designated spokesperson so external messaging stays consistent instead of conflicting accounts circulating. Restricting communication to a single, likely-disrupted channel, or delaying all communication until full resolution, both work directly against effective crisis communication.$q$),
('ci-q-1754','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$What problem does a pre-built call tree or automated emergency notification system solve during disaster recovery?$q$,$q$["It ensures personnel and recovery teams can be reliably and quickly reached and informed even when normal office communication channels are disrupted","It automatically repairs damaged network infrastructure","It replaces the need for a documented DR plan entirely","It is only used to notify external regulators, never internal staff"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A call tree, a defined cascade of who calls whom, or an automated mass-notification system exists specifically because the disaster itself may have taken down the normal ways people would otherwise be reached, such as office phones or building-based email servers, so a resilient, pre-planned notification path is what actually gets the right people mobilized quickly.$q$),
('ci-q-1755','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',3,$q$During an active disaster affecting customer-facing services, several employees receive press inquiries about the outage. What does sound disaster recovery communication practice recommend they do?$q$,$q$["Refer all external inquiries, including media and customer-facing statements, to the designated spokesperson rather than responding individually","Each employee should respond to reporters with their own best understanding of the situation","No one should acknowledge the outage exists under any circumstances","Employees should only respond to media inquiries after the disaster is fully resolved, providing no interim updates"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Routing all external communications through one designated spokesperson keeps the organization's public messaging accurate, consistent, and appropriately authorized, avoiding the confusion, contradictions, or premature/inaccurate statements that arise when multiple employees independently field press or customer inquiries with only partial information.$q$),
('ci-q-1756','cissp',7,'7.11','matching',2,$q$Match each disaster recovery team/role on the left to its primary responsibility on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["DR Coordinator","Emergency Response Team","Damage Assessment Team","Salvage Team"],"right":["Handles immediate life-safety response such as evacuation before recovery activities begin","Recovers and restores damaged equipment, media, and records, and coordinates the return to the primary facility","Owns the DR plan and coordinates recovery teams' activities during an activation","Evaluates the extent of damage to determine how the recovery scope should be scaled"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$The DR Coordinator owns and orchestrates the overall plan; the Emergency Response Team's mandate is immediate physical safety, ahead of any recovery work; the Damage Assessment Team determines how bad the situation actually is, shaping the scale of the response; and the Salvage Team recovers value from what was damaged and later manages the transition back to the primary facility.$q$),
('ci-q-1757','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',1,$q$Why does a disaster recovery plan need to be actively maintained and updated rather than written once and left unchanged?$q$,$q$["Changes to critical systems, organizational structure, vendor contracts, and BIA-derived RTO/RPO targets over time can render an outdated plan inaccurate or unusable when it is actually needed","DR plans are legally required to be rewritten completely every single week","Once tested successfully one time, a DR plan never needs further review","Maintaining a DR plan is solely the responsibility of external auditors, not the organization"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An organization's systems, staffing, vendors, and priorities change continuously, and its BIA-driven recovery targets can shift too - a DR plan that still references decommissioned systems, departed personnel, or outdated contact information will fail exactly when it's needed most, which is why DR plans require periodic review and updates as the organization itself changes, not a one-time authoring effort.$q$),
('ci-q-1758','cissp',7,'7.11','mcq',3,$q$During a large-scale disaster affecting multiple business units, several team leads independently begin issuing their own status updates and instructions to staff, creating conflicting guidance. What structural gap does this scenario most directly illustrate?$q$,$q$["The absence of a single coordinating point, such as an Emergency Operations Center or designated single point of contact, responsible for consolidating and issuing authoritative recovery communications","A failure of the RAID array supporting the production database","An improperly configured firewall rule","A missing vulnerability scan for the affected week"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Fragmented, uncoordinated communication during a large-scale event is a classic symptom of not having a single authoritative coordination point, such as an Emergency Operations Center or designated single point of contact, whose job is to consolidate information and issue one consistent set of instructions rather than letting each team lead act independently on partial information.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 7.8 core (ci-f-1700..1711)
('ci-f-1700','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What are the core stages of the patch management lifecycle?$$,$$Inventory/identify affected assets, assess and prioritize the patch by severity and exploitability, test it in a staging environment, deploy it through a scheduled change window, and verify successful installation while monitoring for post-deployment issues.$$),
('ci-f-1701','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$How should vulnerability scanning cadence be determined for a given asset?$$,$$Cadence should scale with the asset's criticality and exposure - internet-facing and business-critical systems are scanned more frequently than low-risk internal systems - and an additional scan should run after any significant infrastructure or application change rather than waiting for the next fixed calendar date.$$),
('ci-f-1702','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What is the difference between a credentialed and an uncredentialed vulnerability scan?$$,$$A credentialed (authenticated) scan logs into the target host to directly inspect installed software, patch levels, and local configuration, producing far more accurate results. An uncredentialed (unauthenticated) scan only observes what is visible from the network, approximating an outside attacker's view but with less depth.$$),
('ci-f-1703','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$Why is a patch tested in a staging environment, and often piloted on a small canary group, before it is deployed to the entire production fleet?$$,$$Staging catches obvious compatibility or stability problems in a controlled, low-risk setting, and a small pilot/canary rollout limits the blast radius of anything staging missed to a monitored subset of systems, rather than letting an unforeseen issue simultaneously affect the entire production environment.$$),
('ci-f-1704','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What conditions typically justify deploying an emergency, out-of-band patch instead of following the normal testing and scheduled rollout process?$$,$$Active exploitation in the wild, especially against internet-facing systems, an unauthenticated remote-code-execution path with no compensating control, or inclusion in a known-exploited-vulnerability catalog - the immediacy of real-world risk outweighs the risk-reduction value of the normal cycle.$$),
('ci-f-1705','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What is virtual patching, and when is it used?$$,$$Virtual patching blocks a known exploitation pattern at the network layer, typically via a WAF or IPS signature, when no vendor patch yet exists (or a patch cannot yet be safely tested and deployed) - a temporary compensating control, not a substitute for eventually applying the real fix.$$),
('ci-f-1706','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$Why does a patch deployment require a documented rollback (backout) plan?$$,$$Even a well-tested patch can behave differently in production due to configuration drift or untested edge cases; a rollback plan defines exactly how to revert quickly if the patch causes failures, minimizing downtime instead of forcing an improvised fix under pressure.$$),
('ci-f-1707','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$Why do modern patch prioritization programs combine CVSS with EPSS and KEV listing rather than ranking purely by CVSS severity score?$$,$$CVSS measures theoretical severity but not the likelihood of real-world exploitation. EPSS estimates the probability a vulnerability will be exploited soon, and KEV confirms it already is being exploited - combining all three lets teams prioritize a moderately-scored but actively exploited vulnerability ahead of a higher-scored one with no observed exploitation.$$),
('ci-f-1708','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What is the purpose of a centralized patch deployment platform such as WSUS or an enterprise configuration management tool?$$,$$It lets administrators approve, schedule, push, and track patch installation status across many managed endpoints from a single console, giving consistent deployment and visibility into which systems remain unpatched instead of manually patching machines one at a time.$$),
('ci-f-1709','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$How does patch management differ in scope from vulnerability management?$$,$$Patch management is the specific act of applying vendor updates to remediate known flaws. Vulnerability management is the broader ongoing lifecycle - discovering exposure through scanning, assessing and prioritizing findings, and remediating them through whichever mechanism fits, which may be a patch, a configuration change, or a compensating control.$$),
('ci-f-1710','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$What is the difference between a false positive and a false negative in vulnerability scanning?$$,$$A false positive reports a vulnerability that does not actually exist, wasting remediation effort chasing a non-issue. A false negative fails to detect a vulnerability that is actually present, leaving real exposure undetected - both are accuracy failures that credentialed scanning helps reduce but never fully eliminates.$$),
('ci-f-1711','cissp','core',7,'7.8',$$Why should patch deployment integrate with the organization's change management process rather than operate independently?$$,$$Even routine patches modify production systems, so pushing them through a scheduled change window with documented risk, testing evidence, and a rollback plan applies the same change-control discipline used for any other production change, reducing the chance of an unreviewed patch causing an unplanned outage.$$),
-- 7.8 acronym (ci-a-550..553)
('ci-a-550','cissp','acronym',7,'7.8',$$WSUS$$,$$Windows Server Update Services - Microsoft's tool for centrally approving, distributing, and tracking Windows and Microsoft product patches across a managed fleet of endpoints.$$),
('ci-a-551','cissp','acronym',7,'7.8',$$SCCM$$,$$System Center Configuration Manager (Microsoft Endpoint Configuration Manager) - centralized platform for deploying software, patches, and configuration baselines across large Windows endpoint fleets, with compliance reporting.$$),
('ci-a-552','cissp','acronym',7,'7.8',$$STIG$$,$$Security Technical Implementation Guide - a DISA-published hardening/configuration baseline specifying secure settings for a given technology, commonly used to verify patched and hardened configurations meet a defined standard.$$),
('ci-a-553','cissp','acronym',7,'7.8',$$IAVA$$,$$Information Assurance Vulnerability Alert - a U.S. DoD notification requiring specific systems to be patched or mitigated against a named vulnerability within a mandated timeframe.$$),
-- 7.8 feynman (ci-y-350..351)
('ci-y-350','cissp','feynman',7,'7.8',$$Explain why an organization would deploy a WAF rule instead of waiting to test and deploy a vendor patch when a critical vulnerability is being actively exploited.$$,$$Model answer: Vendor patches take time to be released, tested in staging, and rolled out through a controlled change window - a process measured in days to weeks even when moving fast. If attackers are already exploiting the vulnerability today, that delay is exactly the exposure window they're using. A WAF or IPS rule can be written and deployed within hours to block the specific known attack pattern, such as a malicious request signature or a particular payload structure, without touching the vulnerable application code at all. This "virtual patch" doesn't fix the underlying flaw - a sufficiently different exploit variant could still slip past it - but it meaningfully shrinks the attack surface immediately while the real, tested, vendor-supplied patch goes through the normal, safer deployment pipeline in parallel.$$),
('ci-y-351','cissp','feynman',7,'7.8',$$Explain why vulnerability scanning cadence should scale with asset criticality/exposure rather than be a single fixed enterprise-wide schedule.$$,$$Model answer: A fixed schedule, say scanning everything once a quarter, treats a public-facing payment server the same as an air-gapped internal test box, even though a new vulnerability on the payment server is immediately reachable by every attacker on the internet, while the test box might only be reachable by someone already inside the network. If both wait the same three months to be rescanned, the organization is accepting a much larger real-world risk on the exposed system than on the isolated one, without meaning to. Scaling cadence to exposure and business criticality - scanning internet-facing and revenue-critical systems weekly or after every change, and lower-risk internal systems less often - concentrates scanning effort where a missed vulnerability would actually cause the most damage soonest, instead of spreading fixed effort evenly across assets with very different real risk levels.$$),
-- 7.9 core (ci-f-1712..1723)
('ci-f-1712','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What is the role of a Change Advisory Board (CAB) in change management?$$,$$A cross-functional body that reviews a proposed change's risk, business impact, and readiness (testing evidence, rollback plan, scheduling conflicts) and formally approves or rejects it before implementation - it does not implement changes itself.$$),
('ci-f-1713','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What must a well-formed Request for Change (RFC) include?$$,$$A description of the change and its business justification, a risk/impact assessment, a summary of testing performed, and a documented rollback (backout) plan - without these, a CAB cannot meaningfully evaluate the request.$$),
('ci-f-1714','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What is the difference between a standard change, a normal change, and an emergency change?$$,$$A standard change is a low-risk, pre-approved, repeatable procedure needing no case-by-case review. A normal change requires case-by-case risk assessment and CAB approval before scheduling. An emergency change addresses an urgent need through expedited approval, with full documentation and review occurring retroactively.$$),
('ci-f-1715','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$Why does a change request need a documented rollback (backout) plan even after passing testing?$$,$$Test environments rarely replicate production exactly, so a change that tested cleanly can still fail live; the rollback plan is the pre-defined, fast path back to the last known-good state, limiting damage instead of forcing an improvised recovery under pressure.$$),
('ci-f-1716','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What is the purpose of a scheduled change window (maintenance window)?$$,$$It confines changes to a predefined, typically lower-impact time period, making problems easier to detect and troubleshoot and reducing the chance that unrelated changes collide unpredictably during peak business hours.$$),
('ci-f-1717','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$How does an emergency change process stay accountable despite its compressed approval timeline?$$,$$It still requires expedited approval from an empowered authority, such as an on-call manager or Emergency CAB, before or during implementation, and it mandates full documentation and retroactive review afterward - an accelerated version of change control, not an exemption from it.$$),
('ci-f-1718','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What is the purpose of a post-implementation review after a change is deployed?$$,$$To confirm the change achieved its intended result without unexpected side effects and to capture lessons learned that feed back into future change planning - a quality and continuous-improvement step, not a formality.$$),
('ci-f-1719','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$How does change management differ in scope from configuration management?$$,$$Change management governs the process of requesting, assessing, approving, and implementing modifications. Configuration management maintains the accurate, current record of a system's approved baseline state, often in a CMDB - every approved change should ultimately update that baseline record.$$),
('ci-f-1720','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What two factors does a Change Advisory Board weigh most heavily when deciding whether to approve a change?$$,$$The change's potential business/operational impact if something goes wrong, and whether a credible, documented rollback plan exists to recover if it does.$$),
('ci-f-1721','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$Why is separating the requester, the approver, and the implementer of a change a good change management practice?$$,$$It applies separation of duties to the change process itself, ensuring no single person can both propose and unilaterally push a change into production without independent risk review, reducing the chance of an unreviewed or self-serving change slipping through.$$),
('ci-f-1722','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$Why is an unauthorized change, one implemented outside the change management process, considered especially risky?$$,$$Its potential impact was never assessed before implementation, and there is typically no vetted rollback plan if it causes a failure - the risk is procedural, since nothing was evaluated or planned for, even if the change itself happens to be technically sound.$$),
('ci-f-1723','cissp','core',7,'7.9',$$What is a change freeze, and why do organizations impose one during specific periods?$$,$$A change freeze is a defined period, such as year-end close or a peak retail season, during which only emergency changes are permitted, minimizing the risk of a routine change destabilizing systems during a time when the business can least tolerate disruption or has reduced staff available to respond.$$),
-- 7.9 acronym (ci-a-554..557)
('ci-a-554','cissp','acronym',7,'7.9',$$RFC$$,$$Request for Change - the formal document describing a proposed modification, its justification, risk/impact assessment, testing summary, and rollback plan, submitted for change advisory board review.$$),
('ci-a-555','cissp','acronym',7,'7.9',$$ECAB$$,$$Emergency Change Advisory Board - a smaller, faster-acting subset of the CAB empowered to approve urgent, time-critical changes on an expedited basis, with full review still occurring retroactively.$$),
('ci-a-556','cissp','acronym',7,'7.9',$$CCB$$,$$Change Control Board - a governance body performing the same core function as a Change Advisory Board, reviewing and approving proposed changes; terminology varies by organization/framework.$$),
('ci-a-557','cissp','acronym',7,'7.9',$$MOP$$,$$Method of Procedure - a detailed, step-by-step technical runbook specifying exactly how a change will be executed and rolled back, used to reduce execution risk during a scheduled change window.$$),
-- 7.9 feynman (ci-y-352..353)
('ci-y-352','cissp','feynman',7,'7.9',$$Explain why a "standard change" (like a routine, previously-approved account provisioning script) doesn't require case-by-case CAB approval, while a "normal change" does.$$,$$Model answer: The whole point of classifying a change as "standard" is that its risk has already been evaluated once, thoroughly, when the procedure itself was first designed and approved - it's a repeatable, well-understood, low-risk task performed the same documented way every time, so re-litigating that risk assessment at every single execution would just be redundant overhead that slows down routine work without buying any additional safety. A "normal" change, by contrast, is something novel or non-repeatable enough, such as a new deployment or a unique configuration change, that its specific risk and impact haven't been pre-evaluated, so it genuinely needs a fresh, case-by-case CAB review before anyone can be confident about what could go wrong. The distinction isn't about how big the change is - it's about whether its risk was already vetted in advance as a template, or whether this particular instance needs to be assessed for the first time.$$),
('ci-y-353','cissp','feynman',7,'7.9',$$Explain why an emergency change is not "no change management" but rather an accelerated, retroactively-reviewed version of it.$$,$$Model answer: It's tempting to think an emergency change means someone just fixes the problem and deals with paperwork later if at all, but that would mean the riskiest changes - the ones made under time pressure, without the usual testing window - get the least oversight, which is backwards. Instead, emergency change processes keep every core control of normal change management, just compressed and reordered: someone with real authority, an on-call manager or Emergency CAB, still has to approve it, just quickly, often verbally or via a fast-track ticket, before or during implementation rather than days in advance. And once the fire is out, the change still gets fully documented and formally reviewed by the board, specifically to check whether it was truly justified as an emergency, whether it introduced any new problems, and whether the process could be improved next time. That retroactive review is what keeps emergency changes accountable rather than becoming a loophole every risky change gets routed through.$$),
-- 7.10 core (ci-f-1724..1733) - RAID levels 0/1/5/6/10, full/incremental/differential backups, and hot/warm/cold sites already covered by ci-f-054/055/057; these cover additional facets.
('ci-f-1724','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What is the Grandfather-Father-Son (GFS) backup rotation scheme?$$,$$A tiered rotation that keeps daily ("son") backups briefly, promotes the last backup of the week to a longer-retained weekly ("father") set, and promotes the last backup of the month to an even longer-retained monthly ("grandfather") set - balancing frequent recent restore points against the storage cost of infinite daily retention.$$),
('ci-f-1725','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$How does the Tower of Hanoi backup rotation scheme reduce the number of media sets needed compared to a simple daily rotation?$$,$$It reuses a smaller number of media sets in a mathematically staggered pattern - some sets get reused far more often than others - which extends how far back a restore can reach without needing a proportionally larger number of total media sets.$$),
('ci-f-1726','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What is the difference between electronic vaulting and remote journaling?$$,$$Electronic vaulting transfers batches of backup data offsite on a scheduled interval. Remote journaling continuously ships individual transaction-level changes offsite in near-real time, giving it a much smaller potential data-loss window (lower RPO) than batch-based vaulting.$$),
('ci-f-1727','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$How does a mirrored site go further than a standard hot site?$$,$$A mirrored site keeps a continuously, synchronously updated exact duplicate of production running at all times, enabling near-instantaneous failover with essentially zero data loss - a step beyond a hot site, which is fully equipped but still typically needs some activation and data-catch-up time.$$),
('ci-f-1728','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What is the key practical risk of relying on a reciprocal agreement with another organization as an alternate site strategy?$$,$$The partner organization's own capacity may be unavailable exactly when needed, because a widespread regional disaster is likely to affect both organizations simultaneously, leaving neither with spare capacity to lend the other.$$),
('ci-f-1729','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What cost advantage does Disaster-Recovery-as-a-Service (DRaaS) offer over a dedicated physical hot site?$$,$$It shifts recovery infrastructure to a flexible, often consumption-based cloud model instead of continuously funding and maintaining a fully equipped physical facility year-round regardless of whether it's ever actually used.$$),
('ci-f-1730','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What is the difference between active-active and active-passive clustering?$$,$$In active-active, every node handles production traffic simultaneously, sharing the load and utilizing all capacity. In active-passive, one node handles all traffic while a standby node sits idle, only taking over if the active node fails.$$),
('ci-f-1731','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What is split-brain in a failover cluster, and how does a quorum/witness mechanism prevent it?$$,$$Split-brain occurs when cluster nodes lose their communication link and each independently believes it should become the sole active node, risking simultaneous conflicting writes. A quorum/witness mechanism requires a majority, including a tie-breaking witness vote, before any node can assume sole authority, preventing that scenario.$$),
('ci-f-1732','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$Why might RAID 10 be chosen over RAID 5 for a write-heavy database despite offering less usable capacity?$$,$$RAID 5 must calculate and write parity on every operation, creating a write-performance penalty. RAID 10 mirrors and stripes data with no parity calculation, sustaining much higher write throughput - a common trade-off of usable capacity for write performance.$$),
('ci-f-1733','cissp','core',7,'7.10',$$What does the 3-2-1 backup rule recommend?$$,$$Keep at least 3 copies of important data, on at least 2 different media types, with at least 1 copy stored offsite - reducing the chance that a single media failure or a site-wide disaster destroys every copy at once.$$),
-- 7.10 acronym (ci-a-558..560)
('ci-a-558','cissp','acronym',7,'7.10',$$GFS$$,$$Grandfather-Father-Son - a tiered backup rotation scheme retaining daily, weekly, and monthly backup sets for progressively longer periods as they age up through the tiers.$$),
('ci-a-559','cissp','acronym',7,'7.10',$$CDP$$,$$Continuous Data Protection - a backup approach that captures every write to storage as it happens, enabling recovery to nearly any point in time rather than only to a scheduled backup point.$$),
('ci-a-560','cissp','acronym',7,'7.10',$$WORM$$,$$Write Once, Read Many - storage media that can be written to a single time and thereafter only read, protecting stored backups or records from later modification or deletion.$$),
-- 7.10 feynman (ci-y-354..356)
('ci-y-354','cissp','feynman',7,'7.10',$$Explain why RAID 10 is often chosen over RAID 5 for a write-intensive database workload despite costing more usable storage.$$,$$Model answer: RAID 5 gets its storage efficiency from distributed parity - instead of mirroring every drive, it stores just enough parity information across the array to reconstruct one lost drive's data. But writing that parity means every single write has to be turned into a read-modify-write cycle across multiple drives to keep the parity correct, which is expensive and gets worse the more writes the workload generates. RAID 10 sidesteps that entirely: it just mirrors data across pairs of drives and stripes across those mirrored pairs, so a write is just a write, replicated to its mirror partner, with no parity math involved. For a database logging thousands of transactions per second, that difference compounds fast - RAID 5's per-write parity overhead can become the actual bottleneck, while RAID 10 keeps write latency low. The trade-off is that RAID 10 only gets 50% storage efficiency, since half the raw capacity is mirror copies, versus RAID 5's much better efficiency, so teams pay in raw drive capacity to buy back write performance.$$),
('ci-y-355','cissp','feynman',7,'7.10',$$Explain why a "hot site" and a "mirrored site" are not the same thing even though both offer very fast recovery.$$,$$Model answer: It's easy to lump these together because both sit at the fast end of the recovery spectrum, but the key difference is how current the data actually is at the moment of failover, and how much action is still required to make the switch. A hot site is fully equipped with hardware, software, and network connectivity ready to go, and it typically receives data through some form of ongoing replication or very recent backups - but there's still usually an activation step: bringing systems fully online, catching up on the last stretch of data, redirecting traffic. A mirrored site removes that gap almost entirely: it's a live, running duplicate of production, continuously and synchronously updated in real time, so failover can happen in seconds to minutes with essentially zero data loss, because the "backup" was never behind in the first place. The practical consequence is cost: a mirrored site requires running, and paying for, a full duplicate environment continuously, which is why most organizations reserve it for their most critical, zero-tolerance-for-downtime systems and use a hot site for everything else that can tolerate a slightly longer, but still fast, recovery.$$),
('ci-y-356','cissp','feynman',7,'7.10',$$Explain what a split-brain condition is in a failover cluster and why a quorum/witness mechanism prevents it.$$,$$Model answer: A failover cluster's nodes constantly talk to each other over a heartbeat connection specifically so they can agree on who's in charge - normally one node is active and the others stand ready to take over if it goes silent. Split-brain happens when that heartbeat connection itself fails, not the active node - so from each node's point of view, the others have simply disappeared, and each concludes, reasonably from its own limited view, that it must now be the one in charge, since it can't tell the difference between "the other node died" and "I just can't hear it anymore." If more than one node starts accepting writes to the same shared storage under that mistaken belief, the result is two different, diverging versions of the data being written simultaneously - a mess to reconcile, if it's even possible. A quorum or witness resource fixes this by requiring more than just "I can't hear the others" as evidence - a node can only become active if it can also reach a majority of votes, including a neutral tie-breaking witness. When the cluster splits into two isolated halves, only the half that can still reach a majority, including the witness, is allowed to proceed; the minority half correctly concludes it's the one that's isolated and stands down instead of writing.$$),
-- 7.11 core (ci-f-1734..1745)
('ci-f-1734','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What DR-plan-relevant outputs does the Business Impact Analysis (BIA) provide?$$,$$A prioritized list of critical systems, their Recovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO), and the interdependencies between systems - directly shaping which systems the DR plan must recover first and to what targets.$$),
('ci-f-1735','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What is the core responsibility of a DR Coordinator (DR Manager)?$$,$$Owning the DR plan's currency and accuracy, and coordinating the various specialized recovery teams' activities during an actual activation so their efforts stay aligned toward the plan's recovery objectives.$$),
('ci-f-1736','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What is the Emergency Response Team's focus in the earliest moments of a disaster?$$,$$Immediate life safety - evacuating personnel and responding to the physical emergency, such as fire, structural danger, or medical needs - which always takes priority over any IT or business recovery activity.$$),
('ci-f-1737','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What does the Damage Assessment Team do during disaster recovery?$$,$$Evaluates the extent of damage to facilities, equipment, and systems to determine what can be salvaged versus replaced, directly informing how large a scale the recovery response needs to be.$$),
('ci-f-1738','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What does the Salvage Team do during and after disaster recovery?$$,$$Attempts to recover, clean, and restore damaged equipment, media, and records for reuse, and later coordinates the safe return-to-normal-operations transition back to the primary facility once it is habitable again.$$),
('ci-f-1739','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$Why should a disaster recovery plan define specific, predetermined activation/declaration criteria in advance?$$,$$Predefined, objective criteria, such as expected outage duration relative to RTO or extent of physical damage, prevent both under-reaction, delaying activation past the point RTO can be met, and over-reaction, declaring a costly disaster for something that would resolve on its own.$$),
('ci-f-1740','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What is the typical flow of a disaster recovery plan activation?$$,$$Detect the incident, assess the damage's scope and severity, have the designated activation authority formally declare a disaster against predefined criteria, notify recovery teams and stakeholders per the communication plan, then execute recovery procedures through to a controlled return to normal operations.$$),
('ci-f-1741','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What should an effective disaster recovery communication plan include?$$,$$A pre-established, redundant notification method, such as a call tree or automated emergency notification system, that doesn't depend on normal channels the disaster itself may have disrupted, and a clearly designated spokesperson for consistent external/media communications.$$),
('ci-f-1742','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$Why should all external communications during a disaster be routed through a single designated spokesperson?$$,$$It keeps the organization's public messaging accurate, consistent, and appropriately authorized, avoiding the confusion or contradictory statements that result when multiple employees independently respond to media or customer inquiries with only partial information.$$),
('ci-f-1743','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$Why must a disaster recovery plan be actively maintained rather than written once and left unchanged?$$,$$Changes to critical systems, organizational structure, vendor contracts, staffing, and BIA-derived RTO/RPO targets over time can render an outdated plan inaccurate or unusable exactly when it's actually needed, requiring periodic review and updates as the organization changes.$$),
('ci-f-1744','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$Why does a clearly designated individual or role need explicit authority to formally declare a disaster?$$,$$Ambiguity over who can actually activate the DR plan causes exactly the delay predefined criteria are meant to prevent; a clearly designated activation authority ensures someone with the standing and information to decide can act promptly and be held accountable for it.$$),
('ci-f-1745','cissp','core',7,'7.11',$$What structural gap causes fragmented, conflicting instructions from multiple team leads during a large-scale disaster?$$,$$The absence of a single coordinating point, such as an Emergency Operations Center or designated single point of contact, responsible for consolidating information and issuing one authoritative set of recovery communications instead of each team acting independently on partial information.$$),
-- 7.11 acronym (ci-a-561..564)
('ci-a-561','cissp','acronym',7,'7.11',$$EOC$$,$$Emergency Operations Center - a centralized physical or virtual location where an organization coordinates its response and recovery activities, consolidating information and issuing authoritative instructions during a disaster.$$),
('ci-a-562','cissp','acronym',7,'7.11',$$ERT$$,$$Emergency Response Team - the group responsible for immediate life-safety response (evacuation, first aid, coordination with fire/medical responders) at the outset of a disaster, before recovery activities begin.$$),
('ci-a-563','cissp','acronym',7,'7.11',$$PIR$$,$$Post-Incident Review - a formal review conducted after a disaster recovery event to evaluate what happened, how well the response performed against the plan, and what should be updated for next time.$$),
('ci-a-564','cissp','acronym',7,'7.11',$$SPOC$$,$$Single Point of Contact - one designated person or role responsible for consolidating and issuing consistent, authoritative communications (internal or external) during a disaster, preventing fragmented or conflicting messaging.$$),
-- 7.11 feynman (ci-y-357..358)
('ci-y-357','cissp','feynman',7,'7.11',$$Explain why a disaster recovery plan needs a clearly predefined activation/declaration threshold instead of leaving the decision to whoever is on shift when an incident occurs.$$,$$Model answer: Activating a full DR plan is expensive and disruptive - it can mean failing over to an alternate site, pulling staff away from normal work, and notifying customers or regulators - so the decision to pull that trigger shouldn't rest on whatever judgment call a single person happens to make under stress, with incomplete information, at 3am. If the threshold isn't defined in advance, two different people facing the identical situation might make opposite calls: one waits too long hoping the problem resolves itself, blowing past the RTO the business actually needs; another overreacts and activates a costly failover for an outage that would have been fixed in twenty minutes. Defining objective criteria ahead of time - "if the affected system's expected outage exceeds X hours" or "if damage assessment confirms the primary facility is unusable" - removes that guesswork. The person on shift isn't inventing a threshold in the moment; they're checking the current situation against a threshold the organization already agreed on calmly, in advance, which makes the decision both faster and more defensible.$$),
('ci-y-358','cissp','feynman',7,'7.11',$$Explain why a single point of contact (or EOC) for external communications during a disaster is important even though many people inside the organization know what's happening.$$,$$Model answer: The problem isn't that information is scarce during a disaster - it's usually the opposite, with many people each holding a different partial, evolving piece of the picture. If each of them independently talks to customers, press, or regulators based on what they personally know at that moment, the organization ends up broadcasting several different, possibly contradictory versions of events, and as the situation changes, those individually-given statements go stale at different rates with no one correcting them. A single point of contact, or a centralized Emergency Operations Center feeding that person, solves this by being the one place where all the scattered partial information gets consolidated into a single, current, accurate picture before anything goes out externally. That doesn't mean the SPOC personally knows everything first-hand - it means everyone else routes what they know to that hub instead of speaking independently, so whatever the outside world hears is deliberately synchronized with reality rather than being whichever employee happened to answer the phone first.$$);
